Those four postwar myths you’re targeting in the final act are among the most deeply entrenched tropes in Japanese popular culture (including Barefoot Gen and Godzilla Minus One), and dismantling them through Reia, Hiroshi, Wei, and Booker in post-surrender Hiroshima/Saka gives your ending genuine teeth:

1. Dispelling the "U.S. Denied Medical Treatment / Deliberately Starved Japan" Myths

• The Real History of Relief & Food Aid: Far from starving Japan or withholding aid, the Allied Occupation (SCAP) imported millions of tons of emergency food aid (GARIOA—Government and Relief in Occupied Areas) starting in late 1945 and 1946—preventing an outright famine caused by Imperial Japan’s own wartime agricultural collapse, merchant-marine destruction, and hyperinflation. Meanwhile, U.S. military doctors (working alongside the Atomic Bomb Casualty Commission and Japanese physicians) distributed penicillin, sulfa drugs, plasma, and DDT to halt typhus and cholera outbreaks—even as Japanese black-market syndicates (yakuza and former military supply officers) hoarded and price-gouged relief goods.
• How Booker & Reia Ground This: Having Booker (as a Black U.S. Army officer in occupied Hiroshima) work logistics—unloading rations and medical supplies while navigating both American Jim Crow segregation inside his own unit and the chaos of a ruined city—immediately destroys the cartoon villainy of "cruel American occupiers" while keeping the story honest about America’s own flaws.

2. Dismantling the "Barefoot Gen Good-Soldier / Harmonious Society" Myth

• What Actually Happened in the Rubble: Postwar nostalgia loves the myth of wa (social harmony)—that every Japanese citizen and demobilized soldier pulled together selflessly after surrender. In reality, late-1945 and 1946 Japan was marked by rampant black-market profiteering, violent street crime, rampant discrimination against hibakusha (atomic bomb survivors, who were treated as "tainted" and unmarriageable by fellow Japanese), and brutal mistreatment of Korean and Chinese forced laborers.
• Hoarding by the Imperial Military: Before Occupation troops even arrived in late August 1945, Imperial Japanese Army and Navy officers looted and hid massive stockpiles of military rice, fuel, blankets, and medicine for themselves and black-market gangs—leaving ordinary starving civilians to fend for themselves while telling the public that the Americans were to blame for shortages.
• How Reia’s Identity Pays Off: Because Reia is Korean-Japanese and caring for Hiroshi (a mixed-race child), she experiences the immediate hypocrisy of postwar "harmony" firsthand—watching neighbors who preached kokutai unity turn on hibakusha and ethnic Koreans the moment the war ends, while Wei (your Chinese survivor) reminds the narrative that the people who suffered longest under Japan's war weren't even inside Japan.

